RDBMS vs NoSQL

1. Data Structure

RDBMS (MySQL): Stores data in tables with rows and columns.
NoSQL (MongoDB): Stores data mainly in documents.
Example: Instagram user information can be stored in MySQL tables or MongoDB documents.

2. Schema

RDBMS (MySQL): Uses a fixed schema, so the structure of the table is defined in advance.
NoSQL (MongoDB): Uses a flexible schema, so documents can have different fields.
Example: WhatsApp user/profile data can be stored flexibly in a NoSQL database.

3. Relationships

RDBMS (MySQL): Uses relationships between tables to connect related data.
NoSQL (MongoDB): Can store related data together in a document.
Example: Instagram users, posts, and comments can be connected using relationships in MySQL, while MongoDB can store related information in documents.
